  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The Nino 3.4 index is computed as anomalies from each source's own 1950-2014 monthly climatology, and the fragment covers baseline definition, seasonal-cycle removal and the event-threshold exceedance counts.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Each source's anomaly is taken from its **own** 1950-2014 monthly climatology (`compute_enso_nino_index` on model output; `compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`). Do not use `climatology='woa23'` on models, since it puts the model's mean-state bias into the index. State the climatology and baseline used (1950-2014, as the question prescribes).",
        "Match each month to the climatology of its own calendar month, which removes the seasonal cycle. Use calendar months (Jan-Dec) for the phase-locking statistics. Count exceedances of ±1.0 °C as individual months (strict >), as the question defines them, not as persistent events with `min_duration`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "Each source's Nino 3.4 box mask and its ocean cells must be handled consistently when ERSSTv5 and the three models are compared, so the mask applies to the amplitude bias and ranking.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Not applicable as a shared mask: the question asks for a Niño 3.4 index per source, computed from each source's own cells, not for a cell-by-cell metric. Do not apply a common mask to the four fields before the index is computed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "tos is a top-cell bulk potential temperature, not a skin SST, which matters when it is compared with ERSSTv5 SST.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the top-cell bulk temperature, including under ice, so use it directly and do not substitute `thetao`. Each model's index is its anomaly from its **own** climatology, which is what the question asks.",
        "Differences in top-cell thickness (CESM2 0–10 m, GFDL-ESM4 0–2 m, MPI-ESM1-2-LR 0–12 m) are model definitions of SST and not a processing error."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The Nino 3.4 box average of tos must be area-weighted over the surface level, using each model's own published cell areas.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The Niño 3.4 box mean must be area-weighted over valid cells only (`compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`), using each model's own published `areacello`. Mask the weights to the same cells as the data. An unweighted mean on a curvilinear grid is wrong.",
        "The exceedance percentage is a share of months, not of area, so it is a plain count over 780 months. Do not use `compute_exceedance_fraction`, which weights by area."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "model-cesm2-reported-biases",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "datasets/model-cesm2-reported-biases.md",
      "sha256": "869933613b83a939379ed7d3f6510aae802248b39ae0de778138fbd5bc1a5477",
      "reason": "It reports CESM2 ENSO patterns as realistic, which helps interpret the amplitude bias against ERSSTv5.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Context only, not a target: the paper reports ENSO patterns as generally well simulated, with a DJF Niño3.4 El Niño–La Niña composite of 1.38 °C (CAM6) against 1.26 °C (WACCM6). Its reference was ERSSTv5 1920–2017, so it is not the same period."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "model-gfdl-esm4-reported-biases",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "datasets/model-gfdl-esm4-reported-biases.md",
      "sha256": "8ec79c87d10734218374fc56bca956ea159125aeea65b344b1476fb09481f1fe",
      "reason": "It reports a slightly strong, ~4-year ENSO in GFDL-ESM4, which helps interpret its amplitude and seasonal phase locking.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Context only: the paper reports ENSO slightly stronger than observed, with a longer period (~4 years against ~3.3). A GFDL amplitude above ERSSTv5 would be consistent with this. Check it against the computed numbers before asserting it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "The models sit on different grids (CESM2 and MPI native curvilinear, GFDL-ESM4 regridded to 1°×1°), which affects how the 5S-5N, 170W-120W box is selected and weighted.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 is on `gr` (a regridded 1° archive grid) while CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are on `gn`. Use each model's own grid and its own `areacello` for the box mean. Note that GFDL's values are already regridded, and that the coarse MPI grid (250 km) limits equatorial detail."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-values-and-land-cells",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "pitfalls/missing-values-and-land-cells.md",
      "sha256": "f576244f13dde06fed1777eaa9721c68844f61758f66fac704b44f1551e90df2",
      "reason": "Land cells carry the 1e20 missing value, which would dominate the box mean if CF masking were skipped when tos is read.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Use NaN-aware reductions, with weights masked to the valid cells. A value near 1e20 in an index means an unmasked fill value and not a result. Use `temporal_aggregate` for any weighted time aggregation so that land cells stay missing."
      ]
    }
